ad any recent seizures. She is still taking Keppra. She notes that her last seizure was a year ago.\n\nThe patient notes that she is doing well with her depression therapy. She notes that she still feels a little down and stressed. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideation.\n\nRegarding her peptic ulcer disease, she states that she did have a lot of stress a year ago, and she was having some issues. We did an endoscopy which showed gastritis, and she was placed on Protonix. She is doing better now. She denies any issues with this.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills.\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses left elbow pain.\n• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in her hands. Denies weakness in her arm.\n• ENT: Endorses nasal congestion.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left elbow: Erythema and edema of the left elbow. Pain with palpation of the left olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable right radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left arm were obtained. There is no evidence of any elbow fracture.\n\nEndoscopy was performed and showed gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAnna Brown is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with left elbow pain.\n\nOlecranon bursitis.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has left elbow pain. She states that she sits at her desk a lot for work and type and stay in a fixed position all day. After work, it is hard for her to open and close her left elbow. She has noticed some swelling on the tip of her elbow. She is right-handed. She is able to write, which is good, but typing is difficult. She denies numbness or tingling in her hands. She denies any weakness in her arm. She denies any fever or chills. She has erythema and edema of her left elbow. She has pain with palpation of her left olecranon. She is warm to palpation. She has pain with flexion and extension of her left elbow. There is a palpable right radial pulse. X-rays of her left arm were obtained and did not show any evidence of a fracture.\n• Medical Treatment: We will have her use a pillow to support her elbow. We will give her some meloxicam 15 mg, once a day. She can ice the area.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has not had any recent seizures. She is still taking Keppra. Her last seizure was a year ago.\n• Medical Treatment: We will continue with the Keppra 500 mg, twice a day. She needs a refill.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well with her therapy and does not want to take medication.\n• Medical Treatment: We will start her on Lexapro 10 mg, once a day.\n\nPeptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: She did have a lot of stress a year ago and was having some issues. We did an endoscopy which showed gastritis. She was placed on Protonix.\n• Medical Treatment: She will continue Protonix 40 mg, once a day before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAnna Lee is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents today for some joint pain.\n\nThe patient reports left elbow pain. She states she sits at her desk a lot for work and types. She states she has swelling on the tip of her left elbow. She denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness in her hands. She denies any fever or chills.\n\nThe patient reports that she has not had any recent seizures. She has been taking Keppra, and her last seizure was a year ago. She is still taking Keppra. She reports that she went into therapy last year and is doing well. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideations. She is still seeing a therapist and has been doing well. She reports that she is still feeling a little down and stressed.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been doing well with her peptic ulcer disease. She had an endoscopy done a year ago, and it showed that she had an episode of gastritis. She has not had any issues since then, and she is doing well.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses left elbow pain.\n• Neurological: Denies weakness, numbness, or tingling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neck\n\t+ Supple neck without pain or stiffness.\n• Respiratory\n\t+ Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular\n\t+ No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\n• Musculoskeletal\n\t+ Examination of the left elbow: Erythema and edema of the left elbow. Pain to palpation of the olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable left radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left elbow: No evidence of any elbow fracture.\n\nEndoscopy: Revealed gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAnna Lee is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents today for some joint pain.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her epilepsy. Her last seizure was a year ago.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice daily. Prescription refilled today.\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with left elbow pain. She reports sitting at her desk for work and typing. She reports swelling on the tip of her left elbow. She denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness in her hands.\n• Medical Treatment: Rest. Meloxicam 15 mg once daily.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that she is doing well with therapy. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideations.\n• Medical Treatment: Lexapro 10 mg once daily.\n\nPeptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her peptic ulcer disease. She had an endoscopy done a year ago and had an episode of gastritis. She is doing well.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nThe patient is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with some joint pain.\n\nThe patient reports that her left elbow has really been bothering her. She states that she sits at her desk all day for work with her elbows bent. She reports that after work it is really hard for her to flex and extend her left elbow. She reports that it has been really stiff and painful. She also notes that she has noticed swelling on the olecranon of her elbow. She reports that she is right-handed, therefore, she can still write, which is great, but typing is difficult. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands. She reports that she has some weakness in her arm but nothing too significant. She denies any fever or chills.\n\nRegarding her epilepsy, she reports that she has not had any recent seizures. She reports that her last seizure was a year ago. She reports that she is still taking Keppra.\n\nRegarding her depression, she reports that she started therapy last year and she is trying to avoid medications for this problem. She reports that therapy has been helpful, but she still feels down and stressed. She denies suicidal and homicidal ideations.\n\nRegarding her peptic ulcer, she reports that she was dealing with a lot of stress last year. She then underwent an endoscopy which showed that she had an episode of gastritis. She was subsequently diagnosed with peptic ulcer disease and started Protonix. She reports that she has not had any issues with her ulcer since the procedure and everything has been good.\n\nThe patient denies chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting. She reports that she does have a little bit of medial nasal congestion from allergies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Ears, Nose, Mouth and Throat: Endorses medial nasal congestion from allergies.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses left elbow joint pain, swelling, and weakness.\n• Psychiatric: Endorses depression.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left elbow reveals erythema and edema. Pain to palpation of the left olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension. Palpable left radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLeft arm x-ray does not reveal any evidence of elbow fracture.\n\nEGD revealed an episode of gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nAnna Morris is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with some joint pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: I believe she has left olecranon bursitis caused by repetitive trauma.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate meloxicam 15 mg once daily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to use a pillow to rest her arm on. She may also ice the area.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has been doing well on Keppra and denies any recent seizures.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice a day. This was refilled today.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She reports doing well with therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate Lexapro 10 mg once daily.\n\nPeptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her recent endoscopy revealed gastritis\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the