k. The patient has a history of hypertension and has been on Norvasc 5 mg daily, which was increased to 10 mg daily. She reports that her blood pressures have been high recently. She also reports having headaches, which she is unsure if it is due to increased stress at work or her blood pressure. She denies chest pain or shortness of breath. She has a history of a kidney transplant and is on immunosuppressive medications.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Reports high blood pressure. Denies chest pain.\n• Neurological: Reports headaches.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neck\n- General Examination: Supple.\n• Cardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Irregular rate and rhythm. Slight 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n• Respiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear.\n• Abdomen\n- Examination: Left flank scar healed from kidney transplant.\n• Extremities\n- Examination: No lower extremity edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Creatinine: Within normal limits.\n• EKG: Normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient is a 26-year-old female with a history of hypertension and kidney transplant status. Her blood pressure is uncontrolled on Norvasc 10 mg daily. Her creatinine is within normal limits. Her EKG is normal. Her headaches are likely secondary to her hypertension.\n\nHypertension\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is on Norvasc 10 mg daily and her blood pressures are still high.\n• Additional Testing: We will order a lipid panel and a complete metabolic panel.\n• Medical Treatment: We will start the patient on Coreg 25 mg twice a day.\n\nKidney transplant status\n• Medical Reasoning: Her creatinine is within normal limits.\n• Medical Treatment: We will continue her on her current immunosuppressive regimen.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHigh blood pressure.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSusan Johnson is a 26-yea