apy referral . hey dragon , order an autoimmune profile . and then from a hypertension standpoint you , because your blood pressure is a little elevated today , i\'d like you to continue to monitor it at home and , you know , send me a report in the next couple of weeks sh- , you know , giving me the , the blood pressure reportings . do you have a blood pressure cuff at home ?\n[patient] yes , i do .\n[doctor] okay . and then , um , we\'ll go ahead and order a lipid profile for you . hey dragon , order a lipid profile . and then for , from a diabetes standpoint let\'s go ahead and , uh , continue you on your metformin 500 mg twice a day . okay ?\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] do you have any questions ?\n[patient] no , that\'s it .\n[doctor] okay . the nurse will be in soon . it was good to see you .\n[patient] nice seeing you too .\n[doctor] hey dragon , finalize the note .\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJanet Anderson is a 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of joint pain. The onset of her pain began approximately 1 week ago. The patient reports bilateral knee pain. The patient notes that she has been doing stair workouts which she thought would help build muscle on her knees but it has not. The patient denies any other joint pain such as elbows or shoulders. She reports a history of a right rotator cuff repair 2 years ago but denies any problems since that time. She denies any pins and needles in her feet or swelling in her legs. She notes she monitors her blood sugar often due to diabetes but it has been controlled. The patient denies any fever or chills. She denies nausea or vomiting, chest pain, or shortness of breath. She notes that her blood pressure has been elevated but she monitors it and it has not been elevated in any way since the joint pain. She takes Norvasc 5 mg daily and has been taking it every day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills.\nEyes: Denies vision changes.\nHENT: Denies hearing changes.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath or cough.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or palpitations.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting.\nGenitourinary: Denies dysuria or hematuria.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain.\nNeurological: Denies dizziness or weakness.\nEndocrine: Denies heat or cold intolerance.\nPsychiatric: Denies depression or anxiety.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: Supple, no thyromegaly.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination: Soft, non-tender.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Examination of the left knee reveals erythema and edema. There is a small effusion present over the left knee. Decreased flexion and extension of the left knee. Strength in the lower extremities is 5/5.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left knee are reviewed and are normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure was elevated today.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to monitor her blood pressure at home and send me a report in the next couple of weeks. I advised her to continue taking her Norvasc 5 mg daily.\n- Medical Treatment: Lipid profile ordered.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Her A1c was elevated.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue taking her metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n\n3. Left knee pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: Her x-ray of her left knee is normal. She has a significant amount of pain with activity.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to do physical therapy and continue taking anti-inflammatory medication.\n- Medical Treatment: Physical therapy referral. Meloxicam 15 mg once a day. Autoimmune profile ordered.\n\nPatient Agreements: The pati