s.\nEyes: Denies vision changes.\nHENT: Denies hearing changes.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath or cough.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or palpitations.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting.\nGenitourinary: Denies dysuria or hematuria.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain.\nNeurological: Denies dizziness or weakness.\nEndocrine: Denies heat or cold intolerance.\nPsychiatric: Denies depression or anxiety.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: Supple, no thyromegaly.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination: Soft, non-tender.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Examination of the left knee reveals erythema and edema. There is a small effusion present over the left knee. Decreased flexion and extension of the left knee. Strength in the lower extremities is 5/5.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left knee are reviewed and are normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure was elevated today.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to monitor her blood pressure at home and send me a report in the next couple of weeks. I advised her to continue taking her Norvasc 5 mg daily.\n- Medical Treatment: Lipid profile ordered.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Her A1c was elevated.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue taking her metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n\n3. Left knee pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: Her x-ray of her left knee is normal. She has a significant amount of pain with activity.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to do physical therapy and continue taking anti-inflammatory medication.\n- Medical Treatment: Physical therapy referral. Meloxicam 15 mg once a day. Autoimmune profile ordered.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CC:\n\nJoint pain.\n\nHPI\n\nThe patient is a 33-year-old female who presents for the evaluation of joint pain. She reports bilateral knee pain, both equally. She also had a rotator cuff repair 2 years ago. She denies pins and needles in her feet or swelling in her legs. She has diabetes, but her blood sugars have been under control. She also has hypertension, which is currently controlled with Norvasc 5 mg daily. She denies nausea, vomiting, chest pain, shortness of breath, or fever. She notes that she has been doing more stair workouts, which she thought was building muscle on her knees, but it has not helped. She denies pain in her elbows or shoulders.\n\nEXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: Neck is supple without thyromegaly.\n\nCardiovascular\n- General Examination: Regular rate and rhythm.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination: Soft abdomen.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Left knee: Slight erythema and edema. Small effusion present over the left knee. Decreased flexion and extension of the knee. Good strength in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left knee: Normal.\n\nLabs\nWhite blood cell count is within normal limits. A1c is elevated.\n\nIMPRESSION\n\nBilateral knee pain.\nHypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with bilateral knee pain, both equally. She has a history of a right rotator cuff repair 2 years ago. Her knee x-ray is normal.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that she may have a strain in her knees. I recommended that we will do autoimmune labs to work up her left knee pain. I also recommended physical therapy and anti-inflammatory medication to help with the pain. She has been compliant with her Norvasc and metformin.\n- Medical Treatment: We will order an autoimmune profile. She will receive a physical therapy referral