reatment: Increase lisinopril to 10 mg once daily.\n\nDepression\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well on her current regimen.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current treatment.\n\nCarpal tunnel syndrome\n• Medical Reasoning: She reports that her symptoms are doing well.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged her to report any new symptoms such as tingling or numbness.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nFollow up in 1 month. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRoutine yearly exam.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Christine Davis is a 63-year-old female who presents for routine yearly exam. She has a history of hypertension and depression. She also had a carpal tunnel release done approximately 2 years ago.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been doing well with her depression. She has been using the medication that I prescribed her last time and going to therapy weekly. She reports that this has really helped her manage her symptoms.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been sleeping 8 hours a night.\n\nThe patient states that she has been checking her blood pressure, but she states that it has been a little bit up and down lately. She notes that she has not had a normal reading in the last couple of weeks. She states that her blood pressure has been either high or low and has not really stayed put.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been taking her medication. She notes that her diet has been a little bit all over the place lately. She has been stressed with work and putting in more hours, so she has not had time to go to the grocery store, let alone prepare and cook meals. She states that she has been eating a lot of frozen meals.\n\nThe patient notes that she has had some headaches, but she thought that was just from looking at a computer screen all day.\n\nThe patient reports that her carpal tunnel release has been doing well. She states that