ss or swelling.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female with a past medical history significant for alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. She presents today for follow-up of her hypertension.\n\nAlcohol use disorder.\n• Medical Reasoning: She drinks alcohol 4 to 5 times a week to excess.\n• Medical Treatment: She would like to start naltrexone. We previously discussed this. She will take the injectable form and start on 05/17/2021. We reviewed the side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons. Hypertension, she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol. Her target BP is 130 to 140/70 to 80. I will see her back week of 06/14/2021, which is likely around the time of her second naltrexone injection.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: She was started on lisinopril and hydrochlorothiazide at her last visit. Her blood pressure today was 150/100.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nWe will see her back in the week of 06/14/2021. She will receive her first dose of naltrexone on 05/17/2021. She is encouraged to continue her blood pressure checks at home. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CC:\n\nFollow-up hypertension.\n\nHPI:\n\nThe patient is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. Her history is significant for alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nIMMUNIZATIONS:\n\nUpgraded influenza vaccin