 okay . and when was your last pap smear ?\n[patient] uh , about six months ago . it came back normal , though .\n[doctor] okay . good , okay . well , let\'s see ... let\'s take a look at you , and then , we\'ll get some samples for std testing . okay ?\n[patient] all right .\n[doctor] you\'ll hear me talk through your exam , so that i can get it all documented . uh- ?\n[doctor] use my general physical exam template . pelvic exam demonstrates no external lesions . normal labia majora and labia minora . normal physiological discharge , with normal color and smell .\n[doctor] ms. ross , i\'m going to touch you , now . you\'ll feel my fingers , and the speculum in your vagina . you\'ll feel some pressure , now .\n[doctor] cervix is normal . vaginal wall is normal . no cervical motion tenderness and no adnexal tenderness or masses noted . swabs were done for std testing .\n[doctor] okay , ms. ross . everything looks good from a visual standpoint . i\'m going to send off std testing for , uh , gonorrhea , chlamydia , trichomoniasis , and i\'ll have you give some blood for syphilis , hiv and hepatitis c. some of these tests will take longer than others . we will call you , if we see anything , but if you have n\'t heard anything , you can call us in three days for results .\n[patient] okay . sounds good . thank you , so much .\n[doctor] of course , my pleasure . and , if everything is normal , i\'ll see you again , at your next annual checkup .\n[doctor] assessment : is screen for std .\n[doctor] plan : discussed with patient , and please add that she is high risk due to an abnormal pap smear in the past , and the recent discovery of her sexual partner having multiple partners , for some period of time .\n[doctor] end of recording .\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFull spectrum STD testing.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Grace Ross is a 24-year-old female who presents today for full spectrum STD testing. She reports symptoms of discomfort, change in discharge, and odor in her vaginal region. She states that the right side of her vagina is becoming painful. She denies any fevers or chills. She recently learned that her partner has been in sexual encounters with other people and wants to be evaluated for all STDs.\n\nMs. Ross is currently asymptomatic. She started noticing some abnormal discharge last week. She also reports that the discharge smells different and is not the same color or consistency as it has been previously. She also notes that the right side of her vagina has become sore and this has not gone away.\n\nMs. Ross has been dating her partner for 3 years. She states that she is not sure when her partner started cheating on her, but believes it may have been 3 months ago. She has had one irregular pap smear in the past, which was 20 years ago. She has not had one since then. Her last pap smear was 6 months ago and was normal.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of abnormal pap smear.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she is employed as a full-time teacher. She reports that she has been dating her partner for 3 years.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\nGynecologic: Reports abnormal discharge, odor, and right vaginal pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGeneral: Patient is alert and oriented, in no acute distress.\nPelvic Exam: No external lesions. Normal labia majora and labia minora. Normal physiological discharge with normal color and smell. Cervix is normal. Vaginal wall is normal. No cervical motion tenderness. No adnexal tenderness or masses noted. Swabs were done for STD testing.\n\nRESULTS\n\nPending.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nScreen for STD.\n• Medical Reasoning: Patient is high risk due to abnormal pap smear in the past and recent discovery of her sexual partner having multiple partners for some period of time.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of safe sex practices and the risks associated with her partner’s infidelity. We discussed that it is important to get tested regularly for STDs, especially given her history of abnormal pap smears.\n• Medical Treatment: We obtained swabs for gonorrhea, chlamydia, trichomoniasis. We also obtained blood for syphilis, HIV, and hepatitis C. Some of these tests will take longer than others. We will call her if we see anything abnormal, but she can call us in 3 days for results if she has not heard from us.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFull spectrum STD testing.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Ross is a 20 year old female who presents for full spectrum STD testing. She states she is having symptoms of discomfort, a change in discharge, and odor in her vaginal region. The patient states the right side of her vagina is starting to become painful. She denies fevers or chills. She reports that she recently learned that her partner has been in sexual encounters with other people and she wants to be evaluated for all STDs.\n\nMs. Ross states that she started noticing some weird discharge last week. She states the discharge smells different, it\'s not the same color or consistency as it usually is. She states that part did not phase her until she noticed that the right side of her vagina was hurting. She states it is just sore, but that has not gone away.\n\nMs. Ross states she has been dating her partner for 3 years. She states that she thinks he started cheating on her about 3 months ago.\n\nThe patient states she had an abnormal Pap smear in the past, but she has not had one since she was 20. She states she had her last Pap smear about 6 months ago, which was normal.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\nGenitourinary: Reports vaginal discharge, pain, and odor.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the Genitourinary system: External female genitalia normal. No external lesions. 