ght knee pain and swelling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTyler Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents for follow-up of his rheumatoid arthritis. He had a right knee steroid injection last time. He reports that the injection provided relief for about 2 weeks. He notes that he has been having pain in his right knee for the past 2 months. The pain is typically around 6/10, but today it is around 3/10. He notes that his whole leg is swollen. He also reports that he has been slowing down and has found ways to avoid putting pressure on his knee. He notes that the pain is worse when he has been sitting for a while and then tries to stand up. He reports that he is so stiff that he uses a cane to help him get his bearings. He has been taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily. He notes that the Celebrex was started 2 months ago. He also notes that he has been wearing compression socks, but it has not been helping.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain and swelling.\nSkin: Denies rashes.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Normal capillary refill in the toes. Pitting edema around the right knee and ankle.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Range of motion slightly diminished. Palpation of the right knee reveals pitting edema. The right knee is warm to touch.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Rheumatoid arthritis with bilateral knee involvement.\n2. Right knee effusion.\n3. Left knee effusion.\n\nPLAN\n\n1. Administered right knee steroid injection today.\n2. Ordered an X-ray of the right knee.\n3. Recommended physical therapy.\n4. Discussed the possibility of needing a surgical consult if the effusion does not resolve with conservative measures.\n5. Advised the patient to reduce his salt intake.\n6. Advised the patient to prop his feet up when sleeping.\n7. Advised the patient to try to avoid sitting for long periods of time.\n8. Advised the patient to use compression socks.\n9. Discussed the importance of following up with his primary care provider for routine Xeljanz monitoring.\n10. Ordered CMP levels.\n\nPROCEDURE\n\nRight knee steroid injection.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with his primary care physician. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain, swelling, and edema.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTyler Nelson is a 71-year-old male who presents for an orthopedic follow up today. He was seen last in the office 2 months ago for his rheumatoid arthritis. At that time he received a steroid injection in his right knee. He states the injection provided him with relief for a couple of weeks. He notes that his knee is still swollen and painful. He rates his pain level as 6 out of 10. He has been wearing compression socks, but they have not been helpful. He states he has slowed down and has found ways to avoid activities that make his pain worse. He notes that he is stiffer when getting up from sitting. He is using a cane for support. He has been taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily.\n\nMr. Nelson reports that his right knee swelling has been present for 2 months. The swelling has extended to his entire leg. The swelling is not as bad as it was before he started wearing compression socks. He denies any numbness or tingling in his toes. He reports that the right knee is hot to the touch. He denies any redness. He states he can feel a firm mass in his right knee.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nXeljanz 11 mg daily.\nCelebrex 200 mg daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain, swelling, and edema. Endocrine: Denies numbness or tingling in the toes.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Range of motion is slightly diminished. There is pitting edema around the right knee and ankle. Normal capillary refill in the toes. The knee is warm to the touch.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee reveals a degenerative joint disease.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee pain, swelling, and edema.\nRheumatoid arthritis.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an x-ray of the right knee to further evaluate the cause of his swelling. I have also recommended that we refer him to physical therapy. If conservative measures do not improve his symptoms, we may need to consult with an orthopedic surgeon. I have also advised him to reduce his salt intake and elevate his legs when he is sitting or lying down. I have recommended that he follow up with his primary care physician to check his CMP levels. I have also recommended that he continue taking Celebrex 200 mg daily. I have recommended that we perform a steroid injection to the right knee.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with his primary care physician. He will follow up with me in 2 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. He was last seen by me on 03/04/2021, where he received a steroid injection to his right knee. He continues to experience right knee pain and swelling. The pain level is 6/10. He notes when he stands after he has been sitting for a while, he has to use a cane to "get his bearings." The injection he received to the right knee at the last visit was only beneficial for a few weeks. The patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not attended physical therapy.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMusculoskeletal\nLower right extremity: Swollen right knee, slightly reduced range of motion. Skin warm to touch. Lower right leg pits with pressure.\n\nPROCEDURE\n\nFluid aspiration and Steroid injection.\nRisks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to, hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hyperglycemia among others. Verbal consent was obtained. The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach with 3 mL of initially clear fluid then blood-tinged fluid. Then the right knee was prepped in a standard fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg triamcinolone and 1 mL lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well with no complications. Post procedure instructions were given.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis management.\n• High risk medication use.\n• Right knee pain.\n• Lower extremity pitting edema.\n\nPLAN\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\nThe patient will continue Xeljanz XR 11 mg daily. He will also continue Celebrex 200 mg daily.\n\nHigh-risk medication use.\nThe patient is currently on the high-risk medication Xeljanz. We will check his CMP levels and monitor periodically every couple of months.\n\nRight knee pain.\nHe has right knee pain and swelling, with pain level 6/10. On exam today, the right knee continues to be hot, which is ongoing inflammation. We performed a fluid aspiration of the right knee today, see procedure note above. A steroid injection to the right knee was given today, see procedure note above. We will arrange for x-ray of the right knee and refer his to physical therapy.\n\nLower extremity pitting edema.\nThe patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not seen his PCP. We discussed compression stockings, elevate feet, and a low salt diet. He will check with his PCP for further instructions.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?