is\n[patient] no no questions today\n[doctor] okay now the other thing\n[patient] how soon can i how soon will i be able to get up and get moving back outside after the surgery\n[doctor] so the good news is since it\'s a laparoscopic procedure you\'re gon na be back on your feet pretty quick now for the first twenty four hours i\'m gon na want you to take it pretty easy but after a couple of days if you feel like moving around on some solid ground you know we do n\'t want you hiking or anything like that but if you feel like moving around on some solid ground you can get up and walk around within a day or two and then in about a week or so i\'ll see you again and then we\'ll release you to go get back out there in nature\n[patient] okay thank you very much\n[doctor] you\'re very welcome the other thing i wan na talk briefly about is now your not your type two diabetes i want you to follow up with your physician just to make sure that you\'re handling your diabetes especially the fact that you\'re gon na have surgery and those type of things and we will just continue to monitor your diabetes as has been outlined by your your additional either your endocrinologist or your primary care provider i\'m gon na have the nurse come in and get some paperwork signed and if you do n\'t have any questions for me i will see you next week and we will get this gallbladder out\n[patient] well thank you very much i\'m looking forward to i i do n\'t want anymore of that pain so\n[doctor] alright that sounds good we\'ll see you next week then\n[patient] okay take care\n[doctor] yeah\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight upper quadrant abdominal pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBobby Johnson is a pleasant 68-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant abdominal pain. The onset of his pain began several weeks ago. He rates his pain as 8 out of 10. He describes his pain as severe and squeezing in nature. The pain is intermittent and is associated with nausea. The pain is typically worse after meals, but it also occurs 2 hours after meals.\n\nThe patient has a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and has been able to control his blood sugars with diet and exercise. He notes that his diet has improved since his diagnosis with the initiation of a low-carb diet and has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months.\n\nThe patient has a family history of gallbladder disease. His father and mother both had their gallbladder removed.\n\nRegarding his exercise routine, he does not exercise around meals. He has not had any difficulty exercising otherwise.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Endorses low-grade fever.\n• Gastrointestinal: Endorses nausea and vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 128/88 mmHg\nRR: 16\nPulse: 68\nTemp: Afebrile\nSpO2: 99% on room air\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no ectopic beats, no rubs or murmurs noted.\n• Abdomen: Bowel sounds are present. There is slight guarding to the right upper quadrant. No rebound tenderness. Positive Murphy\'s sign. No peritoneal signs.\n\nRESULTS\n\nUltrasound of the abdomen shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. There is mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBobby Johnson is a 68-year-old male who presents today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant abdominal pain.\n\nMild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has not had any improvement in his symptoms with dietary modifications. He has a family history of gallbladder disease and has multiple gallstones present in his gallbladder. He also has mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. There is no evidence of common bile duct obstruction.\n• Medical Treatment: I recommend that we proceed with a laparoscopic cholecystectomy.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week for postoperative care. He will also follow up with his primary care physician for management of his type 2 diabetes mellitus.\n\nThe patient will avoid high-fat foods postoperatively and continue to eat a high-fiber diet. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight upper quadrant pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports he enjoys eating and photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports both parents had gallbladder disease and had cholecystectomy.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports nausea and vomiting.\nHematologic: Denies hematochezia, melena, or hematemesis.\nNeurological: Denies weakness or numbness.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nRespiratory\n- Respiratory rate: 16\n- Oxygen saturation on room air: 99%\n\nCardiovascular\n- Heart rate: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No clicks or murmurs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of the abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Slight guarding present to the right upper quadrant. Murphy\'s sign present. No rebound tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the wall is noted. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been experiencing right upper quadrant pain for several weeks. He has been trying to modify his diet with minimal improvement. He has a family history of cholecystitis.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient today. He is not interested in dietary modifications, so I recommended a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. I explained the procedure and postoperative expectations, including avoiding high fat foods and high fiber diet.\n- Medical Treatment: I will perform a laparoscopic cholecystectomy.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been trying to manage his type 2 diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I recommended he continue to follow up with his primary care provider for continued management of his diabetes. I also recommended he follow up with his endocrinologist.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue current management of his type 2 diabetes.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in one week for a postoperative visit. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Based on the patient\'s symptoms, exam findings, and ultrasound, I do believe he has some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like his gallbladder is inflamed. This appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since he has been symptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I had a thorough discussion with the patient concerning surgical treatment. The preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative courses of care were described and discussed. I explained the surgery will take approximately 1 to 1.5 hours, and he will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We also discussed that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. All questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: Since he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. A mutually agreed upon surgical date has been scheduled for 1 week.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently managing his diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient he should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period; however, he may return to walking outdoors on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\nMedical Treatment: I recommended he follow up with his physician regarding his impending surgery. We will continue to monitor his diabetes as has been outlined by either his endocrinologist or his primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me 1 week status post-surgery.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
