PLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nThe patient is a 28-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for an evaluation of a right knee injury. The patient states that he was playing soccer over the weekend and went to go out and kick the ball and felt a pop in his right knee. This occurred 3 days ago. He states that he noticed some swelling, which has since decreased. He reports that his knee is not as functional as it used to be. He states that he is having pain that is ranging from 6 to 7 out of 10. He denies taking anything for the pain other than ibuprofen, which he has been taking on a regular basis. He reports that he is able to bend his knee but it hurts towards the end of the motion. He denies being able to fully straighten his knee without pain. He states that he is able to bear weight on the knee but it feels loose.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain and swelling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right knee reveals significant effusion. Slight ecchymosis is noted. Pain to palpation on the medial joint line. Positive Lachman test. Muscle strength of 5/5 bilaterally in the lower extremities. Neurovascularly intact distally.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the ACL. I have also recommended that we obtain a CBC to rule out infection. I have prescribed Meloxicam 50 mg once daily for pain and inflammation. I have also recommended that he use ice and a knee immobilizer. Additionally, he should use crutches when ambulating. I have advised him to limit his activities, including his work activities. He should also avoid jogging until further notice. All questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once the MRI results are available. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Thompson is a pleasant 37-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of his pain began 3 days ago, when he was attempting to kick a soccer ball. He reports feeling a pop in his right knee at that time. The swelling has decreased, but he is still experiencing pain, rated between 6-7/10. He has been taking anti-inflammatory medication, but it has provided some relief. He is still able to bend his knee, but it hurts at the end of the motion. He is able to straighten his knee, but it does not feel normal. He is still able to bear weight on the leg, but it feels loose. He reports that he has a limp on his right leg upon ambulation.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been taking meloxicam 50 mg once a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain, swelling, and decreased range of motion.\n\nVITALS\n\nThe patient\'s vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: There is significant effusion in the right knee. Slight ecchymosis is noted. There is pain to palpation at the medial joint line. Passive range of motion is limited, with 115 degrees of terminal extension and 30 degrees of terminal flexion due to pain. Positive Lachman exam.\n\nNEURO: Normal sensation and strength in the right lower extremity.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee sprain.\nRight knee ACL tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination today, I have had a lengthy discussion with him regarding his current symptoms. I have recommended an MRI to evaluate the integrity of the ACL. He will need to moderate his activities. I have prescribed meloxicam 50 mg once a day. I have also recommended icing the knee and using crutches to ambulate. He will need to limit his work activities. He will need to refrain from jogging.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me after he receives the results of his MRI. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Miller is a pleasant 36-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The injury occurred 3 days ago, when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports swelling at the time of the injury, which has improved. The patient states that his knee is not as functional or moving as well as it used to be. He rates his pain level as a 6 to 7 out of 10 currently and a 4 to 5 out of 10 when the swelling decreases. The patient has been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis, which provides some relief. He states that he is able to bend his knee with pain towards the end of this motion; however, when he goes to straighten his knee, he feels like he has to help it straight. The patient adds that he is able to bear weight on his right knee, however it feels loose and does not feel normal.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works at a desk.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Marked limp in the right leg upon ambulation.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Significant effusion. Slight ecchymosis. Pain with palpation at the medial joint line. Difficulty with passive ROM. Lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to 115 degrees. Lacking 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion secondary to pain. Positive Lachman\'s test. Muscle strength is 5/5. Neurovascular intact distally.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee pain, possible anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the ACL. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg a day to reduce swelling and pain. We discussed that the patient should utilize ice and activity modification to reduce swelling and pain. I recommended that he wears a knee immobilizer and use crutches when he is ambulating. He will avoid jogging until we have the results of his MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once the MRI results are available for review and further discussion.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
