exam though there is a three over six systolic ejection murmur heard at the that i\'m hearing at the left base that\'s not like a huge deal especially considering like what you\'ve been through already in terms of the hospital but it is something that i wan na know in your chart when i listen to you your lungs your lungs are clear over here with your extremities i do notice you know some lower lower edema swelling sorry some lower swelling edema lower limb edema and it it is pitting one plus on your right radial artery the cath site is clean dry and intact without hematoma and i also your right radial artery pulses are palpable so i did review the results of your ekg and they show a normal sinus rhythm and a good r wave progression and a a evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a stemi one of the reasons though that we had you come in is that we noticed on your echo there is a reduced ejection fraction of thirty five percent we are a little bit concerned about that so let\'s talk about my assessment and plan for you your primary and like first diagnosis is gon na be coronary artery disease right you already have a history of that but you know we\'re gon na do some things to make we\'re gon na continue to evaluate that over time i want you to continue your aspirin eighty one milligrams daily and your brilinta ninety milligrams twice daily i wan na continue you on a high dose statin called lipitor and you\'re gon na be taking that eighty milligrams a day and we\'re gon na continue you on your toprol and that\'s gon na be fifty milligrams daily i am gon na refer you to cardiac rehab so we can get you some education and give you some confidence to get back to exercising regularly i know it was kinda scary to have such an episode but i promise like most of the patients i have love cardiac rehab and i think you\'ll do pretty well for your second diagnosis we have newly you have newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation what i think is like what that means is that you\'re you\'re pumping like the way that your heart is pumping is a bit concerning but i think like given your history that it will improve over time they got you into the cath lab pretty quickly so i think that the muscle is just kinda like stunned like surprised and since you\'re compliant with your meds i think you will recover we are gon na continue you on your lisinopril twenty milligrams daily however considering that you are retaining fluid i think that your third diagnosis is like we are crossing into acute heart failure i\'m gon na prescribe something called a diuretic it\'s called lasix and i do want to we need to take that forty milligrams once a day i wan na add aldactone twelve . five milligrams daily and i need you to get labs finally we will repeat another echo in about two months last but not least there is the hypertension your blood pressure seems fine i think with the aldactone that you\'ll tolerate that pretty well but we do want to maybe get you into some nutrition counseling to consider like what other diet options might be available to you maybe suggestions of things that you have n\'t thought about like cutting out meat especially any kind of preserved meat like pepperoni but maybe finding you some alternatives how does that sound\n[patient] that sounds good yeah so i\'m yeah as long as you tell me i\'m gon na get better i believe you\n[doctor] yes yeah i i strongly believe this is our best course of action and that you will get better given enough time i i see that you are making efforts i\'m really glad that you are out there walking and that you\'re cognizant of your diet and willing to make changes we will just you know monitor it over time okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] alright thank you\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up after interior ST elevation myocardial infraction (STEMI).\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nThe patient is a 58-year-old female who presents for follow-up after an interior ST elevation myocardial infraction (STEMI) status post placement of an RCA stent in 2018. The patient reports experiencing similar symptoms to her prior myocardial infraction. She denies chest pain or shortness of breath. She is compliant with her medications, including metformin, aspirin, and atorvastatin, but has been less strict with her diet. She states she walks her dog 1.5 to 2 miles daily. She is looking forward to the holiday season.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Reports lower edema.\nRespiratory: Denies dyspnea.\nEndocrine: Denies polyuria.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n- Carotid: No carotid bruits.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ pitting edema bilaterally in the lower extremities.\n\nVascular\n- Examination: Radial artery pulses are palpable. Right radial artery cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma.\n\nRESULTS\n\nElectrocardiogram (EKG): Normal sinus rhythm with R wave progression and evolutionary changes expected post-STEMI.\n\nEchocardiogram: Reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is status post RCA stent placement in 2018 and had a recent interior STEMI. She has a 3/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient on the importance of diet and exercise. She is currently compliant with her medications. We will continue to monitor this over time.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue aspirin 81 mg daily, Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, and Toprol 50 mg daily.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient had an echocardiogram that revealed an ejection fraction of 35%.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed that her ejection fraction is reduced at 35%. I reassured her that this is likely due to the recent myocardial infraction and that she is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Lisinopril 20 mg daily. I will refer her to cardiac rehab. We will repeat an echocardiogram in 2 months.\n\n3. Heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been experiencing lower edema.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of a low-sodium diet. I advised her to avoid preserved meats such as pepperoni.\n- Medical Treatment: Prescribed Lasix 40 mg once daily and Aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure seems fine.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her on the importance of diet and exercise. We will continue to monitor her blood pressure.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nShe will follow up in 2 months for an echocardiogram. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up after interior stemi.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJulia Smith is a 63-year-old female who presents for a follow-up after an interior stemi, which occurred 2 months ago. She had an RCA stent placed at the time. The patient reports that she was doing well until she started experiencing chest pain that would not go away. She went to the emergency room and was told she needed another stent. The patient denies chest pain or shortness of breath. She reports that she has been walking her dog daily for about a mile and a half to 2 miles. She denies any leg swelling. She has noticed that her legs are a little swollen since she was discharged from the hospital. She denies lying flat at night, but states she may sit up a bit more. She states she is able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed. She is watching her salt intake, but is not strict about it. She states that she has been watching her sugar levels and is careful not to go on insulin.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she enjoys walking her dog. She loves pizza and tries. She states she is not watching her salt intake as strictly as she should be.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking aspirin 81 mg daily, Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, and Toprol 50 mg daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea.\n• Respiratory: Denies dyspnea.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies leg swelling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Neck\n\t+ General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n• Cardiovascular\n\t+ Auscultation of the heart: 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n• Respiratory\n\t+ Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n• Musculoskeletal\n\t+ Examination: Bilateral lower extremity edema present, 1+ pitting edema in the right lower extremity. Radial cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nRESULTS\n\nElectrocardiogram reveals normal sinus rhythm with good R wave progression and evolutionary changes as anticipated after a stemi.\n\nEchocardiogram reveals reduced EF of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient had an RCA stent placed 2 months ago and is now experiencing another stemi.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of compliance with medications. I advised her that she will likely recover given her history and compliance with medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. Continue Lipitor 80 mg daily and Toprol 50 mg daily.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: This was noted on her echocardiogram.