cussed that her ejection fraction is reduced at 35%. I reassured her that this is likely due to the recent myocardial infraction and that she is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Lisinopril 20 mg daily. I will refer her to cardiac rehab. We will repeat an echocardiogram in 2 months.\n\n3. Heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been experiencing lower edema.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of a low-sodium diet. I advised her to avoid preserved meats such as pepperoni.\n- Medical Treatment: Prescribed Lasix 40 mg once daily and Aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure seems fine.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her on the importance of diet and exercise. We will continue to monitor her blood pressure.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nShe will follow up in 2 months for an echocardiogram. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up after interior stemi.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJulia Smith is a 63-year-old female who presents for a follow-up after an interior stemi, which occurred 2 months ago. She had an RCA stent placed at the time. The patient reports that she was doing well until she started experiencing chest pain that would not go away. She went to the emergency room and was told she needed another stent. The patient denies chest pain or shortness of breath. She reports that she has been walking her dog daily for about a mile and a half to 2 miles. She denies any leg swelling. She has noticed that her legs are a little swollen since she was discharged from the hospital. She denies lying flat at night, but states she may sit up a bit more. She states she is able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed. She is watching her salt intake, but is not strict about it. She states that she has been watching her sugar levels and is careful not to go on insulin.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she enjoys walking her dog. She loves pizza and tries. She states she is not watching her salt intake as strictly as she should be.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking aspirin 81 mg daily, Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, and Toprol 50 mg daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea.\n• Respiratory: Denies dyspnea.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies leg swelling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Neck\n\t+ General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n• Cardiovascular\n\t+ Auscultation of the heart: 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n• Respiratory\n\t+ Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n• Musculoskeletal\n\t+ Examination: Bilateral lower extremity edema present, 1+ pitting edema in the right lower extremity. Radial cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nRESULTS\n\nElectrocardiogram reveals normal sinus rhythm with good R wave progression and evolutionary changes as anticipated after a stemi.\n\nEchocardiogram reveals reduced EF of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient had an RCA stent placed 2 months ago and is now experiencing another stemi.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of compliance with medications. I advised her that she will likely recover given her history and compliance with medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. Continue Lipitor 80 mg daily and Toprol 50 mg daily.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: This was noted on her echocardiogram.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I reassured her that this is anticipated given her history and that she will likely recover.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: Given her leg swelling.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of compliance with medications and diuretic use. I advised her that she will likely tolerate the diuretic well.\n- Medical Treatment: Start Lasix 40 mg once daily and Aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: She has been tolerating her medications well.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her that she may tolerate the Aldactone well.\n- Medical Treatment: Consider nutrition counseling to explore diet options.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up status post inferior STEMI.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nRCA stent 2018\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\naspirin 81 mg daily\nBrilinta 90 mg twice daily\nLipitor 80 mg daily\nToprol 50 mg daily\nlisinopril 20 mg daily\nmetformin\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral leg swelling.\n\nVITALS\n\nWithin normal limits\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: No carotid bruits. A 3/6 systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left base.\n- Examination: Right radial artery cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Bilateral lower limb edema. Pitting 1+.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG is reviewed and demonstrates a normal sinus rhythm. Good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a STEMI.\n\nEchocardiogram is reviewed and demonstrates a reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of coronary artery disease that we will continue to monitor.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. She will continue Lipitor 80 mg a day and Toprol 50 mg daily. I am going to refer her to cardiac rehab for education and to gain confidence to resume exercising regularly.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: A recent echocardiogram revealed a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. She was able to be treated in the cath lab in a timely manner, so I think that the muscle is just stunned. Given her history. it will improve over time.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that she will recover since she is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is retaining fluid.\n- Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe Lasix 40 mg once a day. I want to add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. I will order lab work. We will obtain a repeat echo in approximately 2 months.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Blood pressure is well controlled. She will tolerate the Aldactone well.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue exercising and to be cognizant of her diet. I explained that there are healthier alternatives available to reduce the amount of preserved meats she consumes.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily. The patient received a referral to nutrition counseling to consider what other diet options might be available to her.\n\n5. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue monitoring her blood glucose levels.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin as prescribed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands an