on na stick with conservative treatment first but we may consider some surgery where i can go in and release that impingement on your nerves through your wrist it\'s a simple outpatient procedure and then i think you would feel better how does that sound\n[patient] that sounds like a good plan i was hoping maybe i could get some relief with some therapy rather than having to go to surgery so i like that plan very much\n[doctor] yeah we\'ll start with the the wrist splint and once i get the nerve conduction studies that will give me another idea whether or not i need to send you some physical therapy in addition or if we need to go directly to surgery but that will all hinge on that nerve conduction test do you so do you have any further questions\n[patient] no that\'s great thank you so much\n[doctor] thank you very much\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight hand numbness and tingling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAlexis Davis is a 46-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right hand numbness and tingling. She reports that she has been experiencing numbness and tingling in her right hand for approximately 6 months. She states that the numbness and tingling is constant and she experiences some pain. The pain is more noticeable when she is typing on the keyboard. She states that taking ibuprofen helps the pain. She also notes that the pain is exacerbated by using the mouse. She denies that the pain wakes her up at night. She states that not typing on the keyboard also helps.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of taking ibuprofen.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she is a computer operator.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right hand pain.\nNeurological: Reports right hand numbness and tingling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Positive Tinel\'s sign on the right.\nMusculoskeletal: Decreased grip strength in the right hand.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nCarpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I have discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. I have recommended that we start with a wrist splint. I have also recommended that she continue with ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day. I have also ordered an outpatient EMG/NCV study to further evaluate the extent of her carpal tunnel syndrome. If the test comes back positive, we will consider surgical intervention.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight hand numbness and tingling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAlexis Rodriguez is a pleasant 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right hand numbness and tingling for the past 6 months. The onset of his symptoms was insidious and has been present on and off for 6 months. The patient reports that over the past month his symptoms have been more persistent. He denies any pain with numbness and tingling. He states that he experiences pain when he is typing on the keyboard. The pain is also exacerbated with mouse use. He has tried using ibuprofen and states that it provides relief. The patient denies that his pain wakes him up at night. He denies any sleep disturbance.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right hand pain with wrist extension and flexion.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right hand reveals pain with wrist extension and flexion. Positive Tinel sign. Decreased grip strength on the right.\n\nRESULTS\n\nNerve conduction studies of the right median nerve were obtained and are pending.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight hand median neuropathy.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. We have decided to start conservative treatment with a wrist splint. I have prescribed ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day. We will order an outpatient median nerve EMG/NCV study. The patient will follow up with me after the test results are in. If the test is positive, we will discuss the possibility of surgery.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me after the EMG/NCV study is complete. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight hand pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAlexis Gutierrez is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right hand pain.\n\nApproximately 6 months ago, the patient began experiencing intermittent episodes of numbness and tingling in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on her right hand. The numbness and tingling has become constant over the past month or so and is accompanied by pain with typing and using a mouse, which she does all day while working on a keyboard. She denies any sleep disturbance associated with her symptoms. Rest and ibuprofen have been the most helpful at providing symptomatic relief.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she works on a keyboard all day.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that she has been taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies sleep disturbance.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports pain in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on the right hand.\nNeurological: Reports numbness and tingling in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on the right hand.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right hand: Pain with flexion and extension of the wrist.. Decreased grip strength on the right compared to the left. Positive Tinel\'s sign.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing her symptoms and exam findings, I believe her symptoms are secondary to carpal tunnel syndrome. We discussed the nature of this condition, as well as her treatment options, in detail. I recommend we treat this conservatively with nighttime wrist splinting and ibuprofen, but I want to increase her dose to 600 mg 4 times daily. I\'m also going to order an EMG/Nerve conduction study for further evaluation. If this is positive, we can consider additional treatment with physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
