 daily.\n\n3. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: This is likely secondary to his hypertension.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of dietary changes and adherence to medication.\n- Medical Treatment: Order renal artery ultrasound to rule out secondary causes.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHospital follow-up.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nThe patient is an 82-year-old male with a past medical history significant for hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease who presents for hospital follow-up after an acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.\n\nThe patient states that he has been trying to watch his diet as instructed. He notes that he has noticed that his ankles have been swelling and that one day he became very short of breath and even had to call 911. The patient states that he was placed on oxygen in the ambulance when he arrived at the emergency room. He was diagnosed with heart failure and was discharged home on Bumex 2 mg daily. The patient states that he has been watching his diet, but admits that he has been having some slips. He reports that he is a big cheese lover and enjoys going to parties on the weekends. He also enjoys pizza and chicken wings. He denies chest pain.\n\nThe patient states that he was in the hospital for a couple of days and was given a medication that made him go to the bathroom frequently and an IV water pill. He reports that he was also started on new medications for his blood pressure. He denies shortness of breath or difficulty sleeping. He reports that he is able to get up and move around and has no problem sleeping. He also denies any chest pain.\n\nThe patient states that he has been checking his blood pressure at home with a blood pressure cuff that he purchased. He reports that his blood pressure has been good.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports lower extremity edema.\n\nVITALS\n\n• Blood pressure: 128/72 mmHg.\n• Respiratory rate: 26 breaths per minute.\n• Heart rate: 79 beats per minute.\n• Oxygen saturation: 99%.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Neck\n\t+ General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n• Respiratory\n\t+ Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular\n\t+ Auscultation of Heart: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n• Musculoskeletal\n\t+ Examination: Trace lower extremity edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram reveals a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Congestive heart failure.\n\t* Medical Reasoning: This appears to be secondary to dietary indiscretion and uncontrolled hypertension.\n\t* Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of adhering to a low salt diet. He should continue to watch his diet and avoid salty foods. He should also continue to take Bumex 2 mg daily. He should weigh himself daily and contact me if he gains 3 lbs in 2 days.\n\t* Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily.\n\t* Additional Testing: Referral to a nutritionist for education on diet.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n\t* Medical Reasoning: This is a chronic condition.\n\t* Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily and Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\t* Additional Testing: Renal artery ultrasound.\n\n3. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\t* Medical Reasoning: This is a chronic condition.\n\t* Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily and Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\t* Additional Testing: Labs.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHospital follow-up after acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of h