in if she has chest pain while walking. I also advised the patient to go to the hospital if she experiences shortness of breath or sweating.\n- Medical Treatment: I will refer her to cardiology for further evaluation. I will order a stress test.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of diabetes. She is doing well on metformin 500 mg twice daily. Her last A1c was 7.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue her current exercise regimen. I also advised her to continue her diet and to try to avoid pop and sweet things.\n- Medical Treatment: I will order an A1c today.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of hypertension. She has had elevated blood pressure readings documented by other clinicians.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that she has a trend of elevated blood pressure. I advised her that we will start her on hydrochlorothiazide.\n- Medical Treatment: I will start her on hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nChest pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes and hypertension.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she enjoys walking.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of heart disease.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.\nCardiovascular: Reports chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies muscle weakness.\nNeurological: Denies headaches, weakness, or numbness.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.\nGenitourinary: Denies urinary frequency or urgency.\nSkin: Denies any rash or lesions.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD. No carotid bruits.\n- Neck Supple without midline cerebellar sign.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rhonchi, or rales.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. Regular rate and rhythm. No extra sounds or rubs. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination: Soft abdomen. No tenderness, guarding, or masses.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ lower extremity edema. No calf tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG is within normal limits.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Chest pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 53-year-old female who presents today with chest pain. She has a past medical history significant for diabetes and hypertension. The patient is concerned that her chest pain may be related to her blood pressure. The patient\'s EKG was normal, but her family history is significant for heart disease and she is a diabetic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the nature of her chest pain with the patient today. I explained to her that her EKG did not show any signs of a heart attack. I also explained to her that her chest pain could be a sign of a blockage and I referred her to cardiology. I also discussed the importance of diet and exercise for her diabetes and hypertension. I also explained the side effects of hydrochlorothiazide, but she has no questions.\n- Medical Treatment: I am going to refer her to cardiology for further evaluation of her chest pain. I am going to start her on nitroglycerin for her chest pain. I also prescribed her hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg once daily. I advised her to monitor her blood pressure at home and to enter her readings into her MyChart.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 53-year-old female who presents today with chest pain. She has a past medical history significant for diabetes and hypertension.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the nature of her diabetes with the patient today. I explained to her that her hemoglobin A1c was 7.0 and she is doing well with her diet and exercise. I also explained to her that she needs to continue to monitor her blood sugar levels and to come back in 2 weeks for a follow-up visit.\n- Medical Treatment: I advised her to continue her metformin 500 mg twice daily. I also advised her to continue her eye ophthalmology appointment.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 53-year-old female who presents today with chest pain. She has a past medical history significant for diabetes and hypertension. Her blood pressure has been trending high over the past several months.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the nature of her high blood pressure with the patient today. I explained to her that her blood pressure has been trending high over the past several months. I also explained to her that her hydrochlorothiazide should help with her blood pressure and leg swelling.\n- Medical Treatment: I advised her to continue monitoring her blood pressure at home and to enter her readings into her MyChart. I also advised her to continue her hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg once daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nChest pain on exertion.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a medical history of diabetes type 2. She has not been formally diagnosed with hypertension, but her blood pressures have been trending high.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of heart disease.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies diaphoresis or clamminess.\nCardiovascular: Reports chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies dyspnea.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD, swelling, or carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Assessment of Respiratory Effort:\n- Auscultation of Lungs: