in.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Sophia Williams is a 34-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The onset of her pain began approximately 6 months ago. The patient reports that she is unable to recall any specific event that caused the pain. She describes her pain as a deep, achy pain located behind her kneecaps. She states that she occasionally hears a creaking in her knees. The pain is exacerbated by activities such as getting from a seated position to standing, watching TV, and going up and down stairs. She states that the pain improves with rest. The patient has previously tried ice and an ACE bandage, and occasionally takes ibuprofen or Aleve, but she tries to avoid taking any medications. She reports that she tries to be as active as possible and enjoys running 5 to 6 miles per day, but she is now only able to run half a mile before her knee hurts.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain, creaking, and tenderness.\nGeneral: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Normal strength in the lower extremities.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the bilateral knees: No erythema or ecchymosis. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Negative Lachman, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the bilateral knees was reviewed and revealed no fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nMs. Sophia Williams is a 34-year-old female who presents today for evaluation of bilateral knee pain. Based on her history and physical exam, her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this time, I recommend that the patient continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed for pain. I also recommend that the patient start physical therapy. The physical therapy will focus on lower extremity exercises to increase strength and mobility and correct any incorrect running mechanics.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Rodriguez is a pleasant 28-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The onset of her pain began approximately 6 months ago. The patient is unsure of the specific event that led to the pain. She notes that the pain is deep and achy, located behind her kneecaps. The patient also describes that she can hear a “creaking” in her knees. The pain is aggravated when she gets up from a seated position, stairs, and running. She states that the pain improves after rest. The patient has tried ice and an Ace wrap to alleviate the pain. She also takes ibuprofen and Aleve as needed. The patient is an avid runner and typically runs 5 to 6 miles a day. However, she notes that she can only run half a mile before her leg starts to hurt.\n\nThe patient has no history of previous knee injuries. She has never broken a bone. The patient is employed and works from home.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain. Neurological: Denies any neurological deficits.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Auscultation of the heart: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\nMSK: Examination of the lower extremities: Normal gait. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. Remainder of muscle strength is 5/5 distally. No erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Lachman\'s, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests are all negative bilaterally. Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the bilateral knees were reviewed today and are unremarkable, showing no fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s history, exam, and radiographic findings, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. I have explained that her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. I have recommended that the patient continue to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed. She will also be referred to physical therapy. Physical therapy will assist with increasing lower extremity strength, mobility, and correcting any incorrect running mechanics.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will refrain from running until further notice. She will follow up with me in the near future. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Jackson is a pleasant 30-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. She was referred by her primary care provider. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. She denies any specific injury. The patient describes her pain as a deep, achy pain. She locates her pain to the posterior aspect of her patellae. The patient also reports “creaking” in her knees. She denies any swelling or redness in her knees. Her pain is aggravated by transitioning from a seated position to standing as well as ascending and descending stairs. Her pain is alleviated by rest. The patient has been icing her knees and wrapping her knees with an ACE bandage. She also takes ibuprofen or Aleve occasionally for pain. She states she tries to be as active as she can. She enjoys running outdoors. The patient adds that she would run 5 to 6 miles per day; however, she is unable to go 0.5 miles secondary to the pain. She denies any previous injuries to her knees.\n\nShe works from home.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of an upper extremity fracture when she was in 6th grade.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: R