cal therapy. The physical therapy will focus on lower extremity exercises to increase strength and mobility and correct any incorrect running mechanics.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Rodriguez is a pleasant 28-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The onset of her pain began approximately 6 months ago. The patient is unsure of the specific event that led to the pain. She notes that the pain is deep and achy, located behind her kneecaps. The patient also describes that she can hear a “creaking” in her knees. The pain is aggravated when she gets up from a seated position, stairs, and running. She states that the pain improves after rest. The patient has tried ice and an Ace wrap to alleviate the pain. She also takes ibuprofen and Aleve as needed. The patient is an avid runner and typically runs 5 to 6 miles a day. However, she notes that she can only run half a mile before her leg starts to hurt.\n\nThe patient has no history of previous knee injuries. She has never broken a bone. The patient is employed and works from home.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain. Neurological: Denies any neurological deficits.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Auscultation of the heart: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\nMSK: Examination of the lower extremities: Normal gait. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. Remainder of muscle strength is 5/5 distally. No erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Lachman\'s, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests are all negative bilaterally. Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the bilateral knees were reviewed to